  "summary": "Conga drums, jínetera ruffles and the fierce flirtation of guaguancó.",
  "description": "Rumba is not ballroom: it is the solar courtyard music of Afro-Cuban Havana and Matanzas, where catá sticks, conga drums and claves drive three forms — yambú (elderly sway), guaguancó (the flirt-and-block courtship dance) and columbia (solo male virtuosity). Women in layered ruffled skirts (bata cubana lines) dance the vacunao ritual tease; singers improvise decima-rooted verses; photographers like Celia González captured the color-code of ruffles and polka dots. UNESCO inscribed rumba in 2016 as a living expression of resistance and joy born of freed-slave barrios, and Los Muñequitos de Matanzas keep the canon alive on stage.",
  "culturalContext": "Rumba is Afro-Cuban identity's courtyard voice: born in freed-slave solares, performed in Havana's Callejón de Hamel and Matanzas' Muñequitos lineage, UNESCO-listed in 2016 as resistance-and-joy heritage — its guaguancó flirtation, decima verses and ruffled dress code the African survival inside Cuban-ness.",
